From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Project Luma <builds@project-luma.local>
Date: Thu, 17 Sep 2026 12:30:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] luma: unread badges on dock icons

A dock icon shows an Ember pill with a count when its app has things waiting
for the person, "99+" past 99, or a dot when the app has something new but no
number. A badge never means an app or its background agent is running.

The badge (lumaDockBadges.js, rules and geometry in lumaDockBadgeModel.js):
- A 15px pill, at least 15 wide, 4px padding, Figtree 750 at 9.5px with
  tabular figures, white on #f26a1b; or a 9px dot. Measured against the
  reference 32px icon on the dock's 36px tile: the pill's top edge 5px above
  and its right edge 5px past that icon, the dot 3px past its corner. The
  right edge is fixed, so a wider count grows leftward and never reaches the
  next icon. Sizes follow the icon size and the display scale.
- A 2px ring in the shelf's own surface colour, read from the shelf material
  around the icon (-luma-badge-ring names an opaque ring for Frost and
  Glass), with a soft shadow under it.
- It lives inside the icon, beside the masked artwork: it rides the hover
  lift, the press, drag-to-rearrange (with the source shelf's ring) and the
  launch zoom, and never changes the dock's layout. Top right at every dock
  edge. The dock's viewport now clips along the shelf only when the dock
  scrolls, so badges on the first and last icons are whole.
- It pops (.6 -> 1.18 -> 1 over 340ms) when it appears or its count goes up;
  decreases and removal are instant; no pop while hovered or without
  animations.
- The dock item's accessible name carries it: "Messages, 3 unread" with the
  real number, "Calendar, new activity".

Where counts come from, per app in this order:
1. The standard `badge` value (an unsigned count or "dot") in an app's
   org.projectluma.BackgroundAgent1 Values, read only from agents that
   declare `badge` in `publishes`, only from the current owner of the
   agent's name, and never by starting luma-background or an agent. An agent
   publishing `badge`, including 0, owns its app's badge.
2. com.canonical.Unity.LauncherEntry Update signals (count, count-visible,
   urgent), merged across partial updates and cleared when the sending app
   disconnects. A sandboxed (Flatpak) sender may badge only its own app ID.
The notification-count fallback and a dot for urgent windows exist behind
one-line switches in lumaDockBadgeModel.js and are off.

org.gnome.shell dock-badges-disabled-apps lists app IDs whose badge the
person turned off; the Shell drops their badge and stops listening to their
agent. Do Not Disturb does not hide badges.

Generated against the Shell .103 tree (0145 applied); applies unchanged on
0146 for release .105.
---
